1 "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186410857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6880ebc-9daa-5cd0-b5d7-2100fa039d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410857 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186410858 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43b5ac79-b939-5882-be87-3661f9387201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410858 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186410856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410859 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186410861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410859 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a7b4be3-0